+---
+type: adr
+status: draft
+entities: [ApiToken]
+related:
+  - app/Auth/apiTokens.ts
+  - app/Http/Middleware/apiToken.ts
+  - app/Http/Controllers/ApiTokenController.ts
+  - app/Http/Controllers/ApiPostController.ts
+  - app/Support/DatabaseRateLimitStore.ts
+  - routes/api.ts
+  - src/app.ts
+generated: { by: "claude-code/claude-fable-5-1", at: 2026-09-29T14:27:34.246Z }
+---
+
+# Personal API tokens authenticate the JSON API only
+
+## Context
+
+Authors publish from scripts and asked for credentials they create and revoke
+themselves, in place of replayed login cookies. Ops asked that no single script
+be able to hammer the site.
+
+The framework offers two ways to accept a bearer token: `auth.useTokens()`,
+which makes a token a way to sign in on every route, and
+`createBearerTokenMiddleware()`, which authenticates the routes it is mounted on.
+
+## Decision
+
+- A token is `{id}|{secret}`. `api_tokens` stores the id and a SHA-256 of the
+  secret. The plain text exists only in the `POST /profile/tokens` response.
+- Tokens authenticate the routes under `/api` and nothing else. They are
+  mounted with the bearer middleware rather than `auth.useTokens()`, so a
+  leaked token cannot change the password, mint more tokens, or edit and delete
+  posts through the web routes.
+- `/api` never authenticates from the session, and is exempt from CSRF on that
+  basis. Tokens are created and revoked from the session only.
+- The limit is 60 requests a minute per token, in fixed windows that start at a
+  token's first request. Counters live in the `rate_limits` table, not in
+  memory, so the limit holds across restarts and processes.
+- Requests that fail authentication are answered 401 before the limiter and are
+  not counted: there is no token to count them against.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- A route added under `/api` must sit inside the bearer-token group in
+  routes/api.ts. Outside it, it would be reachable with neither a token nor
+  CSRF protection.
+- Every API request writes twice: the token's `lastUsedAt` and its counter.
+- Tokens carry no scopes and no expiry. The columns exist (`abilities`,
+  `expires_at`) because the framework's store writes them; nothing reads them yet.
+- Failed bearer attempts are not rate limited. The secret is 256 random bits,
+  so guessing is not the concern; load from unauthenticated requests would need
+  an address-based limit in front of the API.
